#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054622 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4dc94edb-007d-5fe5-b9ca-9ed754adc457" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product and Power Rules of Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "5a566673-4529-57b8-87c9-d5a6118e0398" :question/math-academy-id "e-4082" :question/problem "Express $\\log(3)+4\\log(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the second term to obtain\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+4\\log(y)=\\log(3)+\\log(y^4)\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the two terms to get\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+\\log(y^4)=\\log\\left(3y^4\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+4\\log(y)=\\log\\left(3y^4\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054623 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2c2df20-2d53-5c2e-a633-9442e118a6b8" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Quotient and Power Rules of Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "b0e6a6fc-18fa-57d8-9b4a-2fbd1d7fbb9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4083" :question/problem "Express $3\\log(x) - 2\\log(5)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite both terms of the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\log (x) - 2\\log (5) &= \\log (x^{3}) - \\log (5^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\log (x^{3}) - \\log (25)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we can combine the two terms to get\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(x^3\\right) - \\log(25) =\\log\\left(\\dfrac{x^3}{25} \\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log(x) - 2\\log(5) = \\log\\left(\\dfrac{x^3}{25} \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054539 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054624 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22382eaf-2654-5c65-b7ba-ac71015e84b8" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product and Quotient Rules of Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "c6e57845-614f-5be1-8551-d85d4ffc490e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4085" :question/problem "Express $\\ln(2)+\\ln(x)-\\ln(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule for natural logarithms, we can combine the first two terms of the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\ln (2) + \\ln (x) - \\ln (y) = \\ln (2x) - \\ln (y)\n$$\n\nNow, using the quotient rule for natural logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\ln(2x)-\\ln(y) = \\ln \\left(\\dfrac{2x}{y} \\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln(2)+\\ln(x)-\\ln(y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2x}{y}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054542 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054625 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd9426d-8ce3-564d-85c5-4ae82289325d" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product, Quotient, and Power Rules of Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "de20802c-6a7e-5d77-b2ce-26f98dc68b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4095" :question/problem "Express $3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the first term of the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\log (2) + \\log (x) - \\log (4) &= \\log (2^{3}) + \\log (x) - \\log (4) \\\\\n&= \\log (8) + \\log (x) - \\log (4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms to obtain\n\n$$\n\\log (8) + \\log (x) - \\log (4) = \\log (8x) - \\log (4)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log (8x) - \\log (4) &= \\log (\\frac{8x}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\log (2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4) = \\log\\left(2x\\right)\n$$"}]}
